---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../../styles/story.css";
import ReadingModes from "../../../components/ReadingModes.astro";
import Cite from "../../../components/Cite.astro";
import DataTable from "../../../components/story/DataTable.astro";
import DecisionTimeline from "../../../components/story/DecisionTimeline.astro";
import DieselChart from "../../../components/DieselChart.astro";
import StorySources from "../../../components/StorySources.astro";
import StoryState from "../../../components/StoryState.astro";
import StateEvent from "../../../components/StateEvent.astro";
import { publishedState, eventPath } from "../../../lib/state.mjs";
import { event, prices, release, marchSoFar, shortfalls, usShare, ledgerRows, timeline } from "../../../data/fuel-reserves-2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels.mjs";
const state = publishedState().events.get("event-fuel-reserves-2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels");
if (!state) throw new Error("Missing publication selection: event-fuel-reserves-2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels");
if (state.status === "merged") return Astro.redirect(eventPath(state.survivor), 301);
const markers = [
  { date: "2026-02-28", label: "War with Iran", record: "euronews-eu-document-2026-10-08", passage: "attacks against Iran on 28 February" },
  { peak: true, anchor: "end" },
  { date: "2026-10-02", label: "G7 release", anchor: "end", record: "govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02", passage: "coordinated release through the IEA of 100 million barrels (MB) to begin immediately over 4 months" },
];
---
{state.status === "retired" ? <StateEvent view={state} /> : <BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/fuel-reserves/">Fuel reserves</a><span>October 2, 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="fact">Fuel reserves &middot; October 2, 2026 &middot; updated {event.updatedLabel}</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}</p>
      <ReadingModes />
      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#how-it-came">How it came to this</a></li>
          <li><a href="#owed">Who still owes barrels</a></li>
          <li><a href="#sides">The sides</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The Catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#next">What happens next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
        </details>
      </nav>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">The G7's leaders agreed on October 2 to release {release.barrels} barrels of oil from emergency stocks over {release.months}, including a large diesel release in the first 20 days, to ease fuel prices that have climbed since the war with Iran began.<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="coordinated release through the IEA of 100 million barrels (MB) to begin immediately over 4 months, including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /><Cite s="reuters-no-export-ban-2026-10-02" passage="release diesel and crude oil from their emergency reserves" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="shield households and businesses from price shocks" /><Cite s="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" passage="02/23 3.809" /><Cite s="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" passage="2026-Oct 10/05 6.199" /><Cite s="reuters-spr-limits-2026-09-29" passage="up almost 50% since the war began in late February, and diesel prices have surged about 70%" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The barrels are not new. They are what is left of the {release.marchTotal} barrels governments pledged in March and have not finished releasing.<Cite s="doe-spr-release-2026-03-11" passage="coordinated release of 400 million barrels" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled, we will implement our commitments" /><Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="would bring approximately 100 million barrels to the market" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="5">Which countries supply them, and how much will be diesel, is expected to be settled when the board of the International Energy Agency, which coordinates the emergency stocks of {release.members} countries, meets October 14 and 15.<Cite s="reuters-iea-board-2026-10-06" passage="They did not provide a breakdown of the volumes of crude, diesel and other products to be released, however, or say which countries would participate." /><Cite s="doe-spr-release-2026-03-11" passage="32 member nations of the International Energy Agency" /><Cite s="reuters-iea-board-2026-10-06" passage="is expected to decide the details of the stocks release at an October 14-15 board meeting" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="how-it-came">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">How it came to this</h2>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The dates</strong></figcaption>
          <DecisionTimeline entries={timeline} heading="Event" />
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">Fuel made at refineries is where the market is tightest. The agency's head says crude exports from the Middle East have recovered while the flow of refined products remains "severely constrained." In August the Gulf's net diesel exports were about a quarter of their prewar level, and Ukrainian attacks on Russian refineries had nearly halted Russia's fuel exports. Energy Secretary Chris Wright says U.S. refiners are already "running at record highs."<Cite s="iea-omr-2026-09" passage="refined products, where market tightness is now most acute" /><Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="While crude oil exports from the Middle East have recovered significantly, he said, refined-product flows remain severely constrained" /><Cite s="iea-omr-2026-09" passage="averaged 390 kb/d in August, just over a quarter of pre-war levels" /><Cite s="iea-omr-2026-09" passage="a near-halt to product exports following intensified Ukrainian attacks" /><Cite s="cnbc-export-ban-2026-09-30" passage="American refiners are running at record highs" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">U.S. diesel went from {prices.beforeWar} a gallon the week before the war to a record {prices.record}, and was {prices.oct5} on October 5. EU officials say diesel prices in Europe have doubled since late February.<Cite s="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" passage="02/23 3.809" /><Cite s="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" passage="09/21 6.529" /><Cite s="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" passage="2026-Oct 10/05 6.199" /><Cite s="xinhua-itkonen-2026-10-02" passage="diesel prices have doubled" /></p>
        <DieselChart source="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" from="2026-01-05" to="2026-10-05" markers={markers} />
        <p data-layer="narrative">Of the {release.marchTotal} barrels pledged in March, the U.S. share, {release.usMarch}, is all crude. Europe's contributions are mostly refined products, and France and Germany hold some of Europe's largest stocks of them.<Cite s="iea-contributions-table-2026-03-19" passage=",United States,172.196,172.196,0,0,172.196,0" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="in Europe the contributions will primarily take the form of refined oil products" /><Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="boast some of the largest reserves of refined oil products in Europe" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">Washington asked Europe for two things: deliver the March barrels faster, and add more. Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent said Europe should "accelerate delivery on their existing commitments and make additional supplies immediately available." Wright said Europe would announce "new diesel supplies" that would "meaningfully push diesel prices down."<Cite s="bessent-post-2026-10-01" passage="Our European partners should accelerate delivery on their existing commitments and make additional supplies immediately available" /><Cite s="cnbc-export-ban-2026-09-30" passage="about new diesel supplies that'll come to the market. That'll meaningfully push diesel prices down." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On the first, it got a commitment: the agency's member governments backed finishing the March release as soon as possible, diesel first where they can.<Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="accelerating the oil stock releases announced in the Collective Action of March 2026" /><Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="IEA Members also supported the prioritisation of the release of diesel stocks, to the extent possible" /> On the second, it has nothing yet. Germany says "no additional quantities" as of now, EU officials are holding off "pending a clearer IEA assessment," and France has floated {shortfalls.franceExtra} of diesel without a firm offer.<Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="as of now, no additional quantities" /><Cite s="politico-eu-iea-assessment-2026-10-07" passage="pending a clearer IEA assessment of market conditions and security-of-supply risks" /><Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="France on Wednesday signaled it could release 2 million barrels of diesel on top of the quantities agreed in March" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The pressure came with talk of a ban on U.S. diesel exports, which supplied about half of the European Union's diesel imports in August. Trump said, "let's not send out the diesel," and three people close to the talks said France and Germany were told to release diesel or face a possible ban. Macron said Trump made no threat on the leaders' call, and Trump then ruled out a ban.<Cite s="national-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="The IEA said the US supplied roughly half of the EU's diesel imports in August." /><Cite s="rbc-diesel-strategy-2026-10-01" passage="I've said let's not send out the diesel" /><Cite s="reuters-diesel-demand-2026-10-01" passage="or face a potential US diesel export ban, said three people close to the discussions" /><Cite s="national-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="Mr Macron said Mr Trump did not threaten to issue an embargo on US diesel" /><Cite s="reuters-no-export-ban-2026-10-02" passage="he will not authorize an export ban on diesel, hours after G7 countries agreed to release diesel and crude oil from their emergency reserves." /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="owed">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Who still owes barrels</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The countries behind on their March pledges still owe about {marchSoFar.unreleased} barrels. That is more than the {marchSoFar.apparentGap} the totals suggest, {marchSoFar.released} out of {release.marchTotal} by October 2, because some countries released more than they promised, and their extra does not make up for the others' shortfall.<Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="around 325 million barrels of the IEA collective action announced on 11 March have so far been released" /><Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="with some countries having released greater volumes than they initially pledged" /><Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="would bring approximately 100 million barrels to the market" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Governments offer emergency stocks to the market, and companies can take less than is offered. Germany has released {shortfalls.germanyShare} of the oil it promised. It says companies took its first batch and the market sees no need for more, and it attributes the shortfall to weak demand for the barrels.<Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="Of the 2.65 million tons of oil, diesel and jet fuel promised, Germany has so far released only 600,000 tons onto the market." /><Cite s="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" passage="Eine erste Tranche dieser für die Freigabe vorgesehenen Mengen wurde mittlerweile von den Marktteilnehmern abgerufen." /><Cite s="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" passage="Derzeit wird vom Markt keine weitere Freigabe für erforderlich gehalten." /><Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="Germany attributes that to weak demand for the barrels" /> Paris has said privately that it has {shortfalls.franceLeft} left.<Cite s="politico-eu-france-germany-2026-10-07" passage="Paris has indicated privately that it still has 8 million barrels left under the March commitment" /> The United States lends its barrels to oil companies, which return them later with extra barrels on top. It has signed loan contracts for {usShare.awarded} of its {release.usMarch} barrels. For the last {usShare.offer}, companies agreed to borrow only about 500,000, and it offered them again on September 29.<Cite s="reuters-spr-limits-2026-09-29" passage="The US is loaning oil from the SPR to companies that will repay in crude, with premiums of up to 24%." /><Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-03-20" passage="DOE will move forward with an exchange of 45.2 million barrels of crude oil and receive 55 million barrels in return" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="collectively awarded more than 133 million barrels" /><Cite s="reuters-spr-limits-2026-09-29" passage="In June, the US offered to loan the last 40 million barrels from that agreement, but later that month revealed that companies only agreed to borrow about 500,000 barrels." /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="an exchange of up to 40 million barrels of crude oil" /> Wright says the United States and Japan are delivering while several European countries have released "only a fraction" of their pledges.<Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="several European member countries have released only a fraction of the crude oil and petroleum products they pledged" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The March pledge and what is left of it</strong></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["What is counted", "Barrels"]} rows={ledgerRows} ariaLabel="The March pledge, what has been released, what is still owed and the U.S. share" />
          <p class="chart-source">The agency's counts of October 2 and 7; the U.S. awards are summed from the Energy Department's five award notices, March 20 to June 22.<Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="around 325 million barrels of the IEA collective action announced on 11 March have so far been released" /><Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="would bring approximately 100 million barrels to the market" /><Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-03-20" passage="exchange of 45.2 million barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-04-10" passage="8.5-million-barrels of crude oil" /><Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-04-17" passage="exchange 26 million barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-05-11" passage="approximately 53.3 million barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-award-2026-06-22" passage="500,000 barrels" /></p>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="sides">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">The sides</h2>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">Washington</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Washington says Americans should not pay for a shortage Europe can ease. Bessent said American farmers, truckers and businesses "should not be left carrying the burden of a global diesel shortage," and Trump called Europe's diesel "heavily stocked."<Cite s="bessent-post-2026-10-01" passage="should not be left carrying the burden of a global diesel shortage" /><Cite s="trumpstruth-post-2026-10-02" passage="Europe has just agreed to release a massive amount of their heavily stocked Diesel Oil." /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">Europe</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Europe says further releases depend on what the market needs, and it pushed back on the ban talk: the European Commission said a U.S. ban "would undermine our trust in the United States as a reliable partner."<Cite s="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" passage="Derzeit wird vom Markt keine weitere Freigabe für erforderlich gehalten." /><Cite s="politico-eu-iea-assessment-2026-10-07" passage="pending a clearer IEA assessment of market conditions and security-of-supply risks" /><Cite s="xinhua-itkonen-2026-10-02" passage="It would undermine our trust in the United States as a reliable partner." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Both sides back the same channel: the barrels go through the agency, and the G7 countries recommitted to not restricting energy exports to one another.<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="coordinated release through the IEA of 100 million barrels" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The Catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>"Successfully negotiated with Europe to release 100 million barrels of refined diesel"</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="4">That is the White House's claim, in its fact sheet of October 5.<Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-fact-sheet-2026-10-05" passage="President Trump successfully negotiated with Europe to release 100 million barrels of refined diesel" /> The barrels were already promised: the G7 presents them as carrying out the March pledge, about {marchSoFar.unreleased} barrels of which had not been released.<Cite s="iea-member-meeting-2026-10-07" passage="would bring approximately 100 million barrels to the market" /> The G7 text has "G7 members and partners" releasing, not Europe alone, and the leaders named neither the countries nor a split between crude and diesel.<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="within the first 20 days by G7 members and partners" /><Cite s="reuters-iea-board-2026-10-06" passage="They did not provide a breakdown of the volumes of crude, diesel and other products to be released, however, or say which countries would participate." /> The meeting itself was France's, convened by Macron.<Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="At the invitation of President Emmanuel Macron of France, which holds the 2026 G7 Presidency" /></p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="next">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happens next</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The White House says the release will bring diesel prices down. Prices still rose while the March barrels went out: about {marchSoFar.released} of them were out by October 2.<Cite s="whitehouse-diesel-fact-sheet-2026-10-05" passage="This will increase supply and bring diesel prices down for Americans." /><Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="around 325 million barrels of the IEA collective action announced on 11 March have so far been released" /><Cite s="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" passage="03/16 5.071" /><Cite s="eia-diesel-price-2026-10-06" passage="09/21 6.529" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The reason, an analyst at Saxo Bank says, is that the stress is "no longer crude availability" but "refined product supply, constrained by reduced refinery capacity."<Cite s="reuters-settle-2026-10-02" passage="the main stress in the energy market is no longer crude availability, with Middle East flows recovering, but rather refined product supply, constrained by reduced refinery capacity" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="5">The head of the oil trader Vitol expects tenders<Cite s="reuters-iea-board-2026-10-06" passage="Russell Hardy, chief executive of Vitol, said at a conference in London on Tuesday" />, calls for bids on the barrels, over the next two weeks, and says they "will tell us exactly how much oil and in which location it's going to come out."<Cite s="reuters-iea-board-2026-10-06" passage="those tenders will tell us exactly how much oil and in which location it's going to come out" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="records">
      <StorySources event={event.slug} />
      <StoryState view={state} supporting />
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>}
